00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.100
Imagine you bought a consumer discretionary ET flas week

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.260
when inflation data first printed hotter than expected.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.340
Your order failed at the open,

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.120
but what actually happened was a rapid rotation

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.320
out of growth names into defensives

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.620
as the yield curve steepened and PMI reading softened.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:39.640
The mechanism is straightforward.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.080
During expansion, cyclicals like industrials

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.060
and consumer discretionary historically outperform

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.300
by 4% to 8% annualized.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.360
At the peak, staples and utilities take the lead

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.720
because earnings visibility matters more

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:54.820
than revenue growth.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.180
Professionals always check the 10 year minus 2 year yield spread

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:03.340
and the ISM new orders index before reallocating.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.060
The biggest mistake with sector rotation

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:09.560
is treating it as a timing tool instead of a risk overlay.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:14.760
Instead, always keep a core 60% in broad market ETFs

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.860
and use only 20% to 30% satellite sleeves

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.060
that your balance when the yield curve or PMI crosses

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.480
its 200 day average.

